sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa03-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because males are stronger and more spirited than females, weapons such as horns, tusks and spurs are found only in males in some species and are more fully developed in males in others (e.g. stags have horns and hinds none; hens often lack the spurs of cocks).";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Weapons such as horns and spurs are confined to or larger in males";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book III, Part 1; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: animals with horns, tusks or spurs: deer, cattle, sheep, spurred birds"
}
